<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Winthrop the Younger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1606">1606</a>–<a href="/years/1676">1676</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-winthrop">John Winthrop</a> the Younger was the son of the Massachusetts governor John Winthrop. In 1635, as governor for the patentees led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-fiennes-1st-viscount-saye-and-sele">Lord Saye and Sele</a> and Lord Brooke, he sent men to build a fort at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a>, the start of Saybrook Colony. Long governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> Colony, he won a royal charter in 1662 that also absorbed the New Haven Colony. He was a founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-winthrop-the-younger.jpg" alt="John Winthrop the Younger" width="320" height="375" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Winthrop,_the_Younger_-_Harvard_17th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
